# user/bootsync.ad — MAKE THE NEXT BOOT USE THE MODULES THAT ARE ON THE DISK.
#
# THE HOLE THIS FILLS, STATED FIRST BECAUSE IT IS THE WHOLE POINT
# ==============================================================
# `hpm update` upgrades the driver packages on an installed machine: after it
# runs, /lib/modules/<kver>/kernel/drivers/nvme/host/nvme.ko on the ext4 root
# really is the new file, byte for byte. AND THE NEXT BOOT DOES NOT USE IT.
#
# user/linuxinit.ad:load_modules reads /etc/modules and loads every path in it
# BEFORE the root switch (`bind '#sysroot' /`), so the files it opens are the
# INITRAMFS's, not the disk's. The initramfs is a section of the unified kernel
# image at /boot/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI, which user/hlinstall.ad copied onto this
# machine's ESP byte for byte at install time and which nothing in this tree
# has ever regenerated. So an upgraded nvme.ko governs `modprobe` from the
# moment it lands and has NO EFFECT WHATSOEVER on the next boot -- and on the
# owner's laptop the boot-time set is nvme (to find the disk at all),
# usb-storage (to read the stick), psmouse and i2c-hid (to have a pointer).
# Those are exactly the modules that could not be updated.
#
# HOW THIS PROGRAM CHANGES THAT WITHOUT BUILDING A UKI
# ====================================================
# It does NOT build a boot image. There is no objcopy on this machine, no EFI
# stub, no cpio writer and (by default) no Debian namespace -- and even the
# Debian namespace ships neither binutils nor cpio. Building a UKI here is not
# on the table.
#
# It does not have to be. The Linux initramfs loader accepts a CONCATENATION of
# archives and the LATER one wins: every path in the second archive replaces
# the file the first one unpacked. That is the same mechanism the microcode
# initrd has used for a decade. So the whole job is:
#
#     append a small, UNCOMPRESSED newc cpio archive carrying the CURRENT bytes
#     of the boot modules, and tell the EFI stub the initrd is that much longer.
#
# Uncompressed, because the loader takes a plain cpio segment as happily as a
# gzipped one and this machine then needs no compressor in the path that must
# not fail. MEASURED, not assumed: tests/linux/uki_initrd_slack.sh boots a UKI
# under OVMF with such an overlay appended and watches linuxinit load the
# overlay's list instead of the image's.
#
# THE SPACE IT WRITES INTO IS RESERVED AT BUILD TIME, AND THAT IS THE SAFETY
# =========================================================================
# scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh over-allocates the .initrd section: the section's
# SizeOfRawData covers the real archive PLUS a reservation, while its
# VirtualSize -- the length the EFI stub actually hands the kernel -- is set
# back to the real archive. So the reservation is self-describing
# (slack = SizeOfRawData - VirtualSize) and, more importantly, the bytes this
# program writes are OUTSIDE what the current boot reads.
#
# THE FILE'S LENGTH NEVER CHANGES. Nothing is created, nothing is extended, no
# cluster is allocated, the FAT chain is never mutated and the directory entry
# is never rewritten. That is the identical argument user/bootlogd.ad makes for
# the preallocated boot log, for the identical reason: FAT32 has no journal, and
# the failure to survive is the power button. Every write here is O_SYNC.
#
# THE COMMIT IS FOUR BYTES
# ========================
#   1. VirtualSize is set back to BASE -- the length of the archive the image
#      shipped with. One 4-byte write. From this instant the machine boots the
#      PRISTINE image it was installed with. Stale, and bootable.
#   2. The overlay is written into the reservation. Beyond VirtualSize, so no
#      boot reads it. Interrupt anywhere in here and step 1 is what survives.
#   3. The overlay is READ BACK and compared against the very files it came
#      from. A mismatch stops here, leaving the pristine image.
#   4. VirtualSize is set to BASE + overlay. One 4-byte write, inside one
#      512-byte sector, of which in practice two bytes change.
#
# There is no state between 1 and 4 that boots something incoherent. The only
# way to get one is to tear a single 4-byte write inside a single sector.
#
# WHAT A FAILURE LOOKS LIKE TO SOMEONE WITH NO SHELL: the machine boots, the
# desktop comes up, and the modules are the ones it was installed with. That is
# the SAME outcome as never having run this at all. There is no screen to read
# and nothing to fix, which is the point -- the alternative designs all have a
# window in which the answer is a machine that does not boot.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
# ================================
# IT DOES NOT CHANGE THE BOOT MODULE LIST. The list comes from /boot/UKI.MAP,
# written when the medium was built, and is the list the shipped initramfs
# already has. Only the BYTES behind those names are refreshed. The machine's
# own /etc/modules on the ext4 root grows every time a driver package's install
# hook appends to it (scripts/hamlinux_packages.py:module_install_hook), and
# promoting those additions to BOOT modules is a different and much riskier
# change: it would start loading a GPU driver before the root switch on a
# machine that has never done so. That is a separate decision for the owner,
# not something this program should make on his behalf while he is not looking.
#
# IT DOES NOT TOUCH /boot/vmlinuz OR /boot/initramfs.cpio.gz. Those are the
# rescue copies scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh puts on the ESP; nothing boots them.
#
# Usage:
#   bootsync              refresh the boot modules from the root filesystem
#   bootsync -n           say exactly what it would do; write nothing
#   bootsync --status     print the .initrd geometry and stop
# Env: none. Paths are fixed, because a path this program got wrong is a
#      machine that does not boot.

extern def sys_open(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_open_sync(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_read(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_write(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_close(fd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_lseek(fd: int32, offset: int64, whence: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_fd_size(fd: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_errstr(buf: Ptr[uint8], nbuf: uint64) -> int32

SEEK_SET: int32 = 0

# THE TWO PATHS, AS FUNCTIONS RATHER THAN GLOBALS, AND THAT COST A BOOT TO
# FIND. They were `UKI_PATH: Ptr[char] = cast[Ptr[char]]("/boot/...")` at module
# scope, and on a real installed disk every open through them failed with the
# program's own "cannot read /boot/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI" while `cksum
# </boot/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI` typed at the same shell in the same boot read all
# 67 787 264 bytes of it. A string literal in a GLOBAL INITIALISER does not
# survive to runtime here; one in an expression does. The failure was
# indistinguishable from a missing ESP -- which is exactly the kind of
# success-shaped wrong answer that would have had somebody looking at the mount.
# THE IMAGE THIS PROGRAM REWRITES IS NO LONGER ALWAYS THE ONE FIRMWARE RUNS.
#
# On the original layout \EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI IS the unified kernel image, and
# that is still what this resolves to. On the A/B layout
# (scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh, HAMLINUX_AB_SLOTS=1) that file is systemd-boot --
# a bootloader with no kernel and no .initrd in it -- and the kernel images sit
# beside it as \EFI\Linux\hamnix-a.efi and \EFI\Linux\hamnix-b.efi, with
# \loader\loader.conf naming which one boots.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL. Pointed at the old path on an A/B machine, this
# program does not corrupt anything -- read_pe finds no .initrd section and it
# refuses by name, which is the honest outcome. But refusing is not working:
# the boot MODULE bytes are the one part of the boot image that already updates
# today, and an A/B machine whose bootsync always refuses has silently lost
# that. So the active slot is RESOLVED, and the old path is the fallback rather
# than the assumption.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE: this never writes loader.conf and never
# chooses a slot. It refreshes the modules inside WHICHEVER image this machine
# is currently set to boot. Changing that setting is a different operation with
# a different failure mode, and mixing the two into one program is how a
# module refresh ends up able to make a machine unbootable.
UKI_CAP: uint64 = 256
uki_buf: Array[256, uint8]
uki_resolved: int32 = 0
lconf_buf: Array[1024, uint8]


def uki_store(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and i + 1 < UKI_CAP:
        uki_buf[i] = s[i]
        i = i + 1
    uki_buf[i] = 0


def uki_append(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    n: uint64 = 0
    while uki_buf[n] != 0:
        n = n + 1
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and n + 1 < UKI_CAP:
        uki_buf[n] = s[i]
        n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    uki_buf[n] = 0


# Read \loader\loader.conf and, if it names a default entry that really opens
# under /boot/EFI/Linux, leave that path in uki_buf and return 1.
#
# EVERY FAILURE HERE FALLS BACK rather than stopping: a machine on the original
# layout has no loader.conf at all, and that is not an error, it is the other
# layout.
def resolve_from_loader_conf() -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]]("/boot/loader/loader.conf"))
    if fd < 0:
        return 0
    n: int64 = sys_read(fd, &lconf_buf[0], 1023)
    sys_close(fd)
    if n <= 0:
        return 0
    ln: uint64 = cast[uint64](n)
    lconf_buf[ln] = 0
    # Scan lines for one beginning "default" followed by blanks and a name.
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < ln:
        # start of a line
        s: uint64 = i
        while i < ln and lconf_buf[i] != 10:
            i = i + 1
        e: uint64 = i
        i = i + 1
        while s < e and (lconf_buf[s] == 32 or lconf_buf[s] == 9):
            s = s + 1
        if e - s < 8:
            continue
        if lconf_buf[s] != 100 or lconf_buf[s+1] != 101 or lconf_buf[s+2] != 102 \
           or lconf_buf[s+3] != 97 or lconf_buf[s+4] != 117 or lconf_buf[s+5] != 108 \
           or lconf_buf[s+6] != 116:
            continue
        p: uint64 = s + 7
        if lconf_buf[p] != 32 and lconf_buf[p] != 9:
            continue
        while p < e and (lconf_buf[p] == 32 or lconf_buf[p] == 9):
            p = p + 1
        q: uint64 = e
        while q > p and lconf_buf[q - 1] <= 32:
            q = q - 1
        if q <= p:
            continue
        # A '/' or '\' in the entry name would let a loader.conf reach outside
        # the entry directory. This program opens no such path.
        j: uint64 = p
        while j < q:
            if lconf_buf[j] == 47 or lconf_buf[j] == 92:
                return 0
            j = j + 1
        lconf_buf[q] = 0
        uki_store(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("/boot/EFI/Linux/"))
        uki_append(&lconf_buf[p])
        t: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&uki_buf[0]))
        if t < 0:
            return 0
        sys_close(t)
        return 1
    return 0


def uki_path() -> Ptr[char]:
    if uki_resolved == 0:
        uki_resolved = 1
        if resolve_from_loader_conf() == 0:
            # THE ORIGINAL LAYOUT, and the comment that cost a boot to write:
            # a string literal in a GLOBAL INITIALISER does not survive to
            # runtime here; one in an expression does. On a real installed disk
            # every open through a global failed with this program's own
            # "cannot read /boot/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI" while `cksum
            # </boot/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI` at the same shell in the same boot
            # read all 67 787 264 bytes of it -- a failure indistinguishable
            # from a missing ESP.
            uki_store(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("/boot/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI"))
    return cast[Ptr[char]](&uki_buf[0])


def map_path() -> Ptr[char]:
    return cast[Ptr[char]]("/boot/UKI.MAP")

# /boot/UKI.MAP: first line the decimal BASE (the length of the archive the
# image shipped, which is where the overlay goes), then one absolute .ko path
# per line. 64 KiB is ~250 paths at the length these run to; the image's own
# list is 65 paths and 4.3 KiB.
MAP_CAP: uint64 = 65536
map_buf: Array[65536, uint8]
map_len: uint64 = 0

# One transfer buffer, used for the copy AND for the read-back comparison.
IOBUF: uint64 = 65536
iobuf:  Array[65536, uint8]
vbuf:   Array[65536, uint8]

# A cpio header is 110 bytes plus the name; PATH_CAP bounds the name.
PATH_CAP: uint64 = 512
hdrbuf: Array[1024, uint8]
path_buf: Array[512, uint8]

# PE geometry, filled by read_pe().
pe_sec_hdr:  uint64 = 0        # file offset of the .initrd section header
pe_vsize:    uint64 = 0        # VirtualSize     -- what the stub hands the kernel
pe_rawsize:  uint64 = 0        # SizeOfRawData   -- the reservation ceiling
pe_rawptr:   uint64 = 0        # PointerToRawData

errbuf: Array[160, uint8]


def strlen_u8(s: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
    n: uint64 = 0
    while s[n] != 0:
        n = n + 1
    return n


def puts(fd: int32, s: Ptr[uint8]):
    sys_write(fd, s, strlen_u8(s))


def putdec(fd: int32, v: uint64):
    if v == 0:
        sys_write(fd, "0", 1)
        return
    d: Array[24, uint8]
    i: uint64 = 0
    while v > 0:
        d[i] = cast[uint8]((v % 10) + 48)
        v = v / 10
        i = i + 1
    while i > 0:
        i = i - 1
        sys_write(fd, &d[i], 1)


def why(what: Ptr[uint8]):
    n: int32 = sys_errstr(&errbuf[0], 158)
    puts(2, "bootsync: ")
    puts(2, what)
    if n > 0:
        puts(2, ": ")
        puts(2, &errbuf[0])
    puts(2, "\n")


# --- little-endian scalars out of a byte buffer ---------------------------
def rd_u16(b: Ptr[uint8], o: uint64) -> uint64:
    return cast[uint64](b[o]) + cast[uint64](b[o + 1]) * 256


def rd_u32(b: Ptr[uint8], o: uint64) -> uint64:
    return cast[uint64](b[o]) \
         + cast[uint64](b[o + 1]) * 256 \
         + cast[uint64](b[o + 2]) * 65536 \
         + cast[uint64](b[o + 3]) * 16777216


def read_at(fd: int32, off: uint64, buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int32:
    if sys_lseek(fd, cast[int64](off), SEEK_SET) < 0:
        return -1
    got: uint64 = 0
    while got < n:
        k: int64 = sys_read(fd, &buf[got], n - got)
        if k <= 0:
            return -1
        got = got + cast[uint64](k)
    return 0


# --- the PE section table -------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything this needs is in the first 40 bytes of one section header, and the
# only reason to walk the file at all is to FIND that header. A UKI built by
# scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh puts .initrd last, but nothing here depends on that:
# the table is searched by name.
def read_pe(fd: int32) -> int32:
    hdr: Array[8, uint8]
    if read_at(fd, 60, &hdr[0], 4) != 0:                  # e_lfanew at 0x3C
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("read the DOS header"))
        return -1
    lfa: uint64 = rd_u32(&hdr[0], 0)
    if lfa < 64 or lfa > 4096:
        puts(2, "bootsync: e_lfanew is not plausible; this is not the UKI.\n")
        return -1
    sig: Array[8, uint8]
    if read_at(fd, lfa, &sig[0], 4) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("read the PE signature"))
        return -1
    if sig[0] != 80 or sig[1] != 69 or sig[2] != 0 or sig[3] != 0:   # "PE\0\0"
        puts(2, "bootsync: no PE signature at e_lfanew.\n")
        return -1
    coff: Array[24, uint8]
    if read_at(fd, lfa, &coff[0], 24) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("read the COFF header"))
        return -1
    nsec: uint64 = rd_u16(&coff[0], 6)
    optsz: uint64 = rd_u16(&coff[0], 20)
    if nsec == 0 or nsec > 64:
        puts(2, "bootsync: implausible section count.\n")
        return -1
    tab: uint64 = lfa + 24 + optsz
    i: uint64 = 0
    sh: Array[40, uint8]
    while i < nsec:
        off: uint64 = tab + i * 40
        if read_at(fd, off, &sh[0], 40) != 0:
            why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("read a section header"))
            return -1
        # ".initrd" -- eight bytes, NUL padded.
        if sh[0] == 46 and sh[1] == 105 and sh[2] == 110 and sh[3] == 105 \
           and sh[4] == 116 and sh[5] == 114 and sh[6] == 100 and sh[7] == 0:
            pe_sec_hdr = off
            pe_vsize   = rd_u32(&sh[0], 8)
            pe_rawsize = rd_u32(&sh[0], 16)
            pe_rawptr  = rd_u32(&sh[0], 20)
            return 0
        i = i + 1
    puts(2, "bootsync: this UKI has no .initrd section.\n")
    return -1


# --- newc cpio ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The header is 110 ASCII characters: "070701" then thirteen 8-digit hex
# fields. Uppercase; the kernel's parser takes either.
def hexdig(v: uint64) -> uint8:
    if v < 10:
        return cast[uint8](48 + v)
    return cast[uint8](55 + v)                      # 'A' is 65 = 55 + 10


def put_hex8(buf: Ptr[uint8], o: uint64, v: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < 8:
        buf[o + 7 - i] = hexdig(v % 16)
        v = v / 16
        i = i + 1


# Build a newc header for `name` (namelen bytes, NUL not counted) with
# `fsize` bytes of data, into hdrbuf. Returns the header+name+padding length.
def cpio_header(name: Ptr[uint8], namelen: uint64, fsize: uint64,
                mode: uint64, ino: uint64) -> uint64:
    hdrbuf[0] = 48
    hdrbuf[1] = 55
    hdrbuf[2] = 48
    hdrbuf[3] = 55
    hdrbuf[4] = 48
    hdrbuf[5] = 49                                   # "070701"
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 6,  ino)                    # c_ino
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 14, mode)                   # c_mode
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 22, 0)                      # c_uid
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 30, 0)                      # c_gid
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 38, 1)                      # c_nlink
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 46, 0)                      # c_mtime
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 54, fsize)                  # c_filesize
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 62, 0)                      # c_devmajor
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 70, 0)                      # c_devminor
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 78, 0)                      # c_rdevmajor
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 86, 0)                      # c_rdevminor
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 94, namelen + 1)            # c_namesize (with NUL)
    put_hex8(&hdrbuf[0], 102, 0)                     # c_check (unused by newc)
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < namelen:
        hdrbuf[110 + i] = name[i]
        i = i + 1
    hdrbuf[110 + namelen] = 0
    n: uint64 = 110 + namelen + 1
    while (n % 4) != 0:
        hdrbuf[n] = 0
        n = n + 1
    return n


# --- the write side -------------------------------------------------------
#
# `wfd` is the UKI open O_WRONLY|O_SYNC on an EXISTING file: no O_CREAT, no
# O_TRUNC, and every seek is absolute. The file's length is never touched.
def write_at(wfd: int32, off: uint64, buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int32:
    if sys_lseek(wfd, cast[int64](off), SEEK_SET) < 0:
        return -1
    done: uint64 = 0
    while done < n:
        k: int64 = sys_write(wfd, &buf[done], n - done)
        if k <= 0:
            return -1
        done = done + cast[uint64](k)
    return 0


# THE COMMIT, and the only four bytes that decide which image boots.
def set_vsize(wfd: int32, v: uint64) -> int32:
    b: Array[4, uint8]
    b[0] = cast[uint8](v % 256)
    b[1] = cast[uint8]((v / 256) % 256)
    b[2] = cast[uint8]((v / 65536) % 256)
    b[3] = cast[uint8]((v / 16777216) % 256)
    return write_at(wfd, pe_sec_hdr + 8, &b[0], 4)


zeros: Array[4, uint8]


# EVERY OFFSET BELOW IS RELATIVE TO THE SECTION, AND pe_rawptr IS ADDED AT THE
# I/O CALL. It was not, once, and the result is worth writing down because it
# was the most dangerous shape of wrong answer this whole design exists to keep
# out. The overlay went to file offset BASE instead of PointerToRawData + BASE,
# which is 18 MB INTO the live compressed archive, and the machine
#
#   * BOOTED,
#   * loaded all 64 modules,
#   * ran the boot rc, brought up the network and reached the desktop,
#
# with `Initramfs unpacking failed: junk within compressed archive` sitting at
# t=1.17 s in a kernel log nobody was reading. The kernel had unpacked
# everything up to the corruption and everything needed happened to be before
# it. A corrupted boot image that works is worse than one that does not, so
# tests/linux/bootsync_installed.sh asserts that line is ABSENT from the boot
# AFTER a sync, and check_archive_start below refuses to write at all unless
# PointerToRawData really does point at the start of an archive.


# Does the .initrd section begin with something that is actually an archive?
# gzip (1f 8b) is what scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh puts there; a bare "070701" is
# accepted too, because the loader takes an uncompressed first segment and a
# future image might ship one. Anything else means the geometry read out of the
# PE is not what this program thinks it is, and the correct response to that is
# to write nothing.
def check_archive_start(rufd: int32) -> int32:
    m: Array[8, uint8]
    if read_at(rufd, pe_rawptr, &m[0], 6) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("read the start of .initrd"))
        return -1
    if m[0] == 31 and m[1] == 139:                       # 1f 8b -- gzip
        return 0
    if m[0] == 48 and m[1] == 55 and m[2] == 48 and m[3] == 55 \
       and m[4] == 48 and m[5] == 49:                    # "070701" -- newc
        return 0
    puts(2, "bootsync: the .initrd section does not begin with an archive.\n")
    puts(2, "bootsync: PointerToRawData is not where this program thinks it "
            "is, and\n")
    puts(2, "bootsync: writing on that assumption would land inside the boot "
            "image.\n")
    puts(2, "bootsync: Refusing. Nothing was changed.\n")
    return -1


# Copy one file's bytes into the reservation at `at`, preceded by its cpio
# header. Returns the new offset, or 0 on failure (0 can never be a valid
# result: `at` starts at BASE, which is never zero).
def emit_file(wfd: int32, at: uint64, path: Ptr[uint8], plen: uint64) -> uint64:
    rfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](path))
    if rfd < 0:
        why(path)
        return 0
    sz: int64 = sys_fd_size(rfd)
    if sz < 0:
        sys_close(rfd)
        why(path)
        return 0
    fsize: uint64 = cast[uint64](sz)
    # The cpio name is the path WITHOUT its leading '/': archives are relative
    # to the root they unpack into.
    hn: uint64 = cpio_header(&path[1], plen - 1, fsize, 33188, at)
    if write_at(wfd, pe_rawptr + at, &hdrbuf[0], hn) != 0:
        sys_close(rfd)
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("write a cpio header"))
        return 0
    p: uint64 = at + hn
    left: uint64 = fsize
    while left > 0:
        want: uint64 = IOBUF
        if want > left:
            want = left
        k: int64 = sys_read(rfd, &iobuf[0], want)
        if k <= 0:
            sys_close(rfd)
            why(path)
            return 0
        if write_at(wfd, pe_rawptr + p, &iobuf[0], cast[uint64](k)) != 0:
            sys_close(rfd)
            why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("write module bytes"))
            return 0
        p = p + cast[uint64](k)
        left = left - cast[uint64](k)
    sys_close(rfd)
    pad: uint64 = (4 - (p % 4)) % 4
    if pad > 0:
        if write_at(wfd, pe_rawptr + p, &zeros[0], pad) != 0:
            why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("write cpio padding"))
            return 0
        p = p + pad
    return p


# THE READ-BACK, which is the assertion that makes the commit honest. It
# compares the bytes now sitting in the boot image against the bytes of the
# file they were taken from -- not against a length, and not against a log
# line. If hpm laid down a new nvme.ko, this is what proves the boot image
# carries THAT nvme.ko.
def verify_file(rufd: int32, at: uint64, path: Ptr[uint8],
                plen: uint64) -> uint64:
    rfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](path))
    if rfd < 0:
        why(path)
        return 0
    sz: int64 = sys_fd_size(rfd)
    if sz < 0:
        sys_close(rfd)
        return 0
    fsize: uint64 = cast[uint64](sz)
    hn: uint64 = cpio_header(&path[1], plen - 1, fsize, 33188, at)
    if read_at(rufd, pe_rawptr + at, &vbuf[0], hn) != 0:
        sys_close(rfd)
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("read back a cpio header"))
        return 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < hn:
        if vbuf[i] != hdrbuf[i]:
            sys_close(rfd)
            puts(2, "bootsync: the cpio header read back WRONG for ")
            puts(2, path)
            puts(2, "\n")
            return 0
        i = i + 1
    p: uint64 = at + hn
    left: uint64 = fsize
    while left > 0:
        want: uint64 = IOBUF
        if want > left:
            want = left
        k: int64 = sys_read(rfd, &iobuf[0], want)
        if k <= 0:
            sys_close(rfd)
            return 0
        if read_at(rufd, pe_rawptr + p, &vbuf[0], cast[uint64](k)) != 0:
            sys_close(rfd)
            why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("read back module bytes"))
            return 0
        j: uint64 = 0
        while j < cast[uint64](k):
            if vbuf[j] != iobuf[j]:
                sys_close(rfd)
                puts(2, "bootsync: the boot image does NOT match ")
                puts(2, path)
                puts(2, "\n")
                return 0
            j = j + 1
        p = p + cast[uint64](k)
        left = left - cast[uint64](k)
    sys_close(rfd)
    p = p + (4 - (p % 4)) % 4
    return p


# --- /boot/UKI.MAP --------------------------------------------------------
def read_map() -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_open(map_path())
    if fd < 0:
        puts(2, "bootsync: no /boot/UKI.MAP on this machine's ESP.\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: that file is written by scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh "
                "and copied by\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: user/hlinstall.ad. Without it the length of the "
                "shipped\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: initramfs is unknown, and GUESSING IT WOULD "
                "OVERWRITE THE BOOT\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: IMAGE. Refusing, and changing nothing.\n")
        return -1
    map_len = 0
    while map_len < MAP_CAP:
        k: int64 = sys_read(fd, &map_buf[map_len], MAP_CAP - map_len)
        if k <= 0:
            break
        map_len = map_len + cast[uint64](k)
    sys_close(fd)
    if map_len >= MAP_CAP:
        puts(2, "bootsync: /boot/UKI.MAP is at or over the 64 KiB cap; "
                "refusing to act on a\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: list that may have been truncated.\n")
        return -1
    if map_len == 0:
        puts(2, "bootsync: /boot/UKI.MAP is empty.\n")
        return -1
    return 0


map_pos: uint64 = 0


# Pull the next non-blank, non-comment line into path_buf, NUL terminated.
# Returns its length, or 0 at the end.
def next_line() -> uint64:
    while map_pos < map_len:
        j: uint64 = 0
        while map_pos < map_len and map_buf[map_pos] != 10:
            if j < PATH_CAP - 1:
                path_buf[j] = map_buf[map_pos]
                j = j + 1
            map_pos = map_pos + 1
        map_pos = map_pos + 1
        while j > 0 and path_buf[j - 1] <= 32:
            j = j - 1
        path_buf[j] = 0
        if j == 0:
            continue
        if path_buf[0] == 35:                        # '#'
            continue
        return j
    return 0


def parse_dec(s: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int64:
    v: uint64 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    if n == 0:
        return -1
    while i < n:
        if s[i] < 48 or s[i] > 57:
            return -1
        v = v * 10 + cast[uint64](s[i]) - 48
        i = i + 1
    return cast[int64](v)


def show_geometry():
    puts(1, "bootsync: .initrd section header at file offset ")
    putdec(1, pe_sec_hdr)
    puts(1, "\n           raw data at ")
    putdec(1, pe_rawptr)
    puts(1, ", reservation (SizeOfRawData) ")
    putdec(1, pe_rawsize)
    puts(1, "\n           in use (VirtualSize) ")
    putdec(1, pe_vsize)
    puts(1, ", free ")
    putdec(1, pe_rawsize - pe_vsize)
    puts(1, "\n")


def streq(a: Ptr[uint8], b: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while a[i] != 0 and b[i] != 0:
        if a[i] != b[i]:
            return 0
        i = i + 1
    if a[i] == b[i]:
        return 1
    return 0


def main(argc: int32, argv: Ptr[uint64]) -> int32:
    dry: int32 = 0
    stat_only: int32 = 0
    ai: int32 = 1
    while ai < argc:
        a: Ptr[uint8] = cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[cast[uint64](ai)])
        if streq(a, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("-n")) != 0:
            dry = 1
        elif streq(a, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("--status")) != 0:
            stat_only = 1
        else:
            puts(2, "bootsync: usage: bootsync [-n] [--status]\n")
            return 2
        ai = ai + 1

    # THE PATH IS PRINTED, NOT SPELLED OUT, and that is not cosmetic: since the
    # A/B layout this may be either \EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI or the slot
    # \loader\loader.conf names, and a refusal that names the wrong file sends
    # whoever reads it to look at the wrong thing.
    upath: Ptr[char] = uki_path()
    rufd: int32 = sys_open(upath)
    if rufd < 0:
        puts(2, "bootsync: cannot read ")
        puts(2, cast[Ptr[uint8]](upath))
        puts(2, ".\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: on an installed machine the ESP is bound at /boot "
                "by\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: etc/rc.boot.installed. Nothing was changed.\n")
        return 1
    if read_pe(rufd) != 0:
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1
    if stat_only != 0:
        show_geometry()
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 0

    if check_archive_start(rufd) != 0:
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1

    if read_map() != 0:
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1

    # The first line is BASE: the length of the archive the image shipped, and
    # therefore the offset the overlay goes at. It is read rather than derived
    # because after one successful run VirtualSize is no longer BASE, and a
    # program that guessed would append to its own last overlay until the
    # reservation ran out.
    n0: uint64 = next_line()
    base_s: int64 = parse_dec(&path_buf[0], n0)
    if base_s <= 0:
        puts(2, "bootsync: the first line of /boot/UKI.MAP is not a length.\n")
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1
    base: uint64 = cast[uint64](base_s)
    if base > pe_vsize or base > pe_rawsize:
        puts(2, "bootsync: /boot/UKI.MAP's base does not fit this boot "
                "image.\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: the map and the UKI are not from the same build. "
                "Refusing.\n")
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1
    if (base % 4) != 0:
        puts(2, "bootsync: the base length is not 4-byte aligned; the loader "
                "would not\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: find the overlay's header. Refusing.\n")
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1

    # --- pass one: SIZE IT, touching nothing --------------------------------
    # A reservation that is too small must be found BEFORE the first byte is
    # written, because the write itself is what shrinks the running image back
    # to base.
    want: uint64 = 0
    count: uint64 = 0
    save_pos: uint64 = map_pos
    while 1 == 1:
        pl: uint64 = next_line()
        if pl == 0:
            break
        if path_buf[0] != 47:
            puts(2, "bootsync: /boot/UKI.MAP holds a non-absolute path.\n")
            sys_close(rufd)
            return 1
        fd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&path_buf[0]))
        if fd < 0:
            puts(2, "bootsync: a boot module named in /boot/UKI.MAP is not on "
                    "this root:\n           ")
            puts(2, &path_buf[0])
            puts(2, "\nbootsync: refusing to build a boot image that is "
                    "missing a driver.\n")
            sys_close(rufd)
            return 1
        sz: int64 = sys_fd_size(fd)
        sys_close(fd)
        if sz < 0:
            sys_close(rufd)
            return 1
        hn: uint64 = cpio_header(&path_buf[1], pl - 1, cast[uint64](sz),
                                 33188, 1)
        want = want + hn + cast[uint64](sz)
        want = want + (4 - (want % 4)) % 4
        count = count + 1
    # the TRAILER!!! record closes the archive
    tn: uint64 = cpio_header(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("TRAILER!!!"), 10, 0, 0, 0)
    want = want + tn

    puts(1, "bootsync: ")
    putdec(1, count)
    puts(1, " boot modules, ")
    putdec(1, want)
    puts(1, " bytes of overlay; reservation ")
    putdec(1, pe_rawsize - base)
    puts(1, " bytes\n")

    if base + want > pe_rawsize:
        puts(2, "bootsync: THE OVERLAY DOES NOT FIT THE RESERVATION.\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: this medium was built with too little slack in "
                "the .initrd\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: section. Nothing has been written and the machine "
                "boots exactly\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: as it did before. Re-image to get a bigger "
                "reservation.\n")
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1

    if dry != 0:
        puts(1, "bootsync: -n given; nothing written.\n")
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 0

    wfd: int32 = sys_open_sync(uki_path())
    if wfd < 0:
        puts(2, "bootsync: cannot open the boot image for writing.\n")
        puts(2, "bootsync: nothing was changed.\n")
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1

    # --- STEP 1: fall back to the shipped image -----------------------------
    if set_vsize(wfd, base) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("shrink .initrd to the shipped length"))
        sys_close(wfd)
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1
    puts(1, "bootsync: the boot image is now the one this machine was "
            "installed with.\n")

    # --- STEP 2: fill the reservation ---------------------------------------
    map_pos = save_pos
    at: uint64 = base
    while 1 == 1:
        pl: uint64 = next_line()
        if pl == 0:
            break
        at = emit_file(wfd, at, &path_buf[0], pl)
        if at == 0:
            puts(2, "bootsync: the overlay was NOT completed. The machine "
                    "boots the image\n")
            puts(2, "bootsync: it was installed with -- unchanged and "
                    "bootable.\n")
            sys_close(wfd)
            sys_close(rufd)
            return 1
    tn2: uint64 = cpio_header(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("TRAILER!!!"), 10, 0, 0, 0)
    if write_at(wfd, pe_rawptr + at, &hdrbuf[0], tn2) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("write the cpio trailer"))
        sys_close(wfd)
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1
    total: uint64 = at + tn2 - base

    # --- STEP 3: read it back against the files it came from -----------------
    map_pos = save_pos
    vat: uint64 = base
    while 1 == 1:
        pl: uint64 = next_line()
        if pl == 0:
            break
        vat = verify_file(rufd, vat, &path_buf[0], pl)
        if vat == 0:
            puts(2, "bootsync: VERIFICATION FAILED. The size field was never "
                    "moved, so the\n")
            puts(2, "bootsync: machine boots the image it was installed with. "
                    "Nothing is\n")
            puts(2, "bootsync: half-applied.\n")
            sys_close(wfd)
            sys_close(rufd)
            return 1
    if vat != at:
        puts(2, "bootsync: the read-back ended at a different offset than the "
                "write.\n")
        sys_close(wfd)
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1
    puts(1, "bootsync: ")
    putdec(1, count)
    puts(1, " modules verified in the boot image against the files on this "
           "root\n")

    # --- STEP 4: THE COMMIT --------------------------------------------------
    if set_vsize(wfd, base + total) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("commit the new initrd length"))
        puts(2, "bootsync: the machine still boots the shipped image.\n")
        sys_close(wfd)
        sys_close(rufd)
        return 1
    sys_close(wfd)
    sys_close(rufd)
    puts(1, "bootsync: committed. The next boot loads these modules.\n")
    return 0
